WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.618
[AUDIO LOGO]

00:00:00.618 --> 00:00:02.910
SPEAKER: Welcome to this
episode of the Click to Deploy

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:06.120
series, where we show you how
easy it is to deploy solutions

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.280
from Google Cloud Marketplace.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.870
In this episode,
I'll show you how

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.960
to set up a MongoDB deployment
through the Google Cloud

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.010
Marketplace.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.310
MongoDB is a NoSQL
document-oriented database

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.970
that stores JSON-like
documents with dynamic schemas.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.470
When you are building
your projects,

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.280
you might be interested in
using MongoDB as your database.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.860
Click to Deploy makes it
easy to quickly spin up

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.840
a MongoDB database instance.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.050
We'll start by searching for
MongoDB in the GCP Marketplace.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.300
As you see, we have a
couple options for MongoDB

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.010
from different vendors.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.860
You can deploy a
container image of MongoDB

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.390
or a virtual machine.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:49.990
For the purpose
of this tutorial,

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:52.680
let's choose the Google
Click to Deploy, a MongoDB

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.420
virtual machine.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.190
We are sent to this
landing page, which

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.130
shows some important
information about MongoDB,

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.920
like an overview
of what MongoDB is,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.230
details about the virtual
machine and its operating

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.680
system, and, most importantly,
the estimated monthly costs

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.300
of choosing this solution.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.220
Let's click the Launch button
to start this deployment.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.950
Now we get to choose some
important specifications

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.300
for our deployment.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.400
We'll start by naming our
deployment for this video.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.570
We will name it MongoDB-101.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:24.770
We'll choose the zone.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.230
For this tutorial, I'll
keep the default zone,

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:28.880
then you can decide
on servers here

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.340
for this deployment, which
is how many servers you

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.450
want to deploy.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:33.830
I'll choose one.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.440
Next, you can determine your
machine family and the machine

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.227
type.

00:01:37.227 --> 00:01:39.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.840
For this demo, I'll
use e2 highmem 4,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.970
which is a high-memory
machine type that

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.510
has 8 gigabytes of
system memory per vCPU.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:52.500
We'll keep the defaults
for boot disk type.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.360
You also have the option
to select Arbiter tier.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.530
Arbiter is a MongoDB instance
that is a part of replica set

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.840
but does not hold data,
and is instead used

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.580
to participate in elections.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.490
Finally, you can also decide
on additional specifications

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:15.290
like CPU platform, GPU, network
interface, and firewalls.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.060
Now we've completed
the specification.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.036
We'll click the Deploy button.

00:02:19.036 --> 00:02:22.368
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:24.250
Great!

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.010
So now our MongoDB
VM is deployed,

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.230
and now we see the
Deployment Manager.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.870
The Deployment Manager allows
you to create and manage

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:33.370
cloud resources.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.620
Here we see the deployed
MongoDB instance,

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.860
which includes files that
show VM instance, disk,

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:42.170
and firewall information.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:44.110
Now we can test this
MongoDB instance

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.760
by running a couple of commands.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.225
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.540
So there you have it-- a
MongoDB instance created

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.660
with a click of a button.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.940
If you enjoyed this video,
please subscribe and hit

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.320
the Like button below.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.600
If you'd like to
deploy MongoDB VM,

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.180
we encourage you to check
out the GCP Marketplace

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.230
and deploy the
solution on your own.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:22.150
Thanks for watching.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

